  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1030 PM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nSaturday through Thanksgiving:\n\nSynopsis: Attendant to low pressure moving into the southern Hudson \nBay, a cold front will extend roughly from the Minnesota Boundary \nWaters to the Missouri River Valley by Saturday morning/the \nbeginning of the long term period. Aided along by upper troughs \nmoving into the Ohio Valley & along the US-Canada border, the front \nwill slowly work into and through southern Wisconsin Saturday night \nthrough Sunday morning, bringing widespread rain potential & \nbeneficial accumulations. The front will depart east of the area \nSunday afternoon, allowing a chilly Canadian air mass to advect into \nthe western Great Lakes for the beginning of next week. Canadian \nhigh pressure will build into and through the region during the \nfirst half of next week, keeping the colder air locked in place \nmoving toward the Thanksgiving holiday. Initial indications from the \nNBM point toward a cold, albeit mainly dry Thanksgiving Day in \nsouthern Wisconsin, though trends will continue to be monitored.  \n\nSaturday night into Sunday morning: Still expecting a period of \nwidespread rain as a cold front steadily works across southern \nWisconsin. Available forecast soundings continue to depict an \nenvironment characterized by ~1\" precipitable water values & weak \ninstability in the mid-levels, which would translate to beneficial \nrainmakers with a few harmless rumbles of thunder. Probabilistic QPF \nforecasts continue to hover around the 0.25\" threshold, with initial \nindications pointing toward totals in the 0.10-0.25\" range east of \nthe Kettle Moraine & 0.25-0.50\" totals further west. Will continue \nto refine these ranges & locations as this portion of the period \ndraws closer.  \n\nSunday night through Wednesday: Continue to expect an elongated \nstretch of much colder temperatures, with highs generally in the \nupper 30s-low 40s and overnight lows in the 20s. NBM continues to \nhint at overnight lows in the 10s across the northwest, which \nremains contingent on the verification of colder forecasts from the \nCMC. Will be watching this trend into next week. Plan to grab the \nwinter jacket when heading out during the morning hours early next \nweek. \n\nWednesday night through Thanksgiving: Current forecast from the NBM \nis dry, but will be monitoring the modeled progression/evolution of \nlarge scale troughing across the central CONUS into early next week. \nInitial indications from the LREF point toward the bulk of \naffiliated precip impacts tracking from the Gulf Coast up the \nEastern Seaboard, with lingering high pressure impacts keeping \nsouthern Wisconsin dry. Will continue to provide updates. \n\nQuigley",
